</figure>
<figure id="S0.F1" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2603.12129v1/fig_tech_ladder.png" id="S0.F1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:429/295;" width="429" height="295" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 1: </span><span id="S0.F1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Technology ladder: system overload across
five levels of population sophistication.</span>
Under abundance
(<math id="S0.F1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C/N&gt;0.6" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0.6</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C/N&gt;0.6</annotation></semantics></math>), the most sophisticated L4/L5 achieve near-zero overload. Under scarcity (<math id="S0.F1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C/N\lesssim 0.5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow><mo>≲</mo><mn>0.5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C/N\lesssim 0.5</annotation></semantics></math>), the cheapest population (L1)
achieves the lowest overload. All five
curves cross near <math id="S0.F1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C/N\approx 0.5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow><mo>≈</mo><mn>0.5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C/N\approx 0.5</annotation></semantics></math>.
L1 and L2 can be calculated analytically; L3–L5
are empirical (20 seeds <math id="S0.F1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\times" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>×</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\times</annotation></semantics></math> 500 rounds,
<math id="S0.F1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pm 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>±</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pm 1</annotation></semantics></math> SE shaded bands).
<span id="S0.F1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Inset:</span> L4 (FRD)–L5 (LOTF) crossover at
<math id="S0.F1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N=15" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>15</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N=15</annotation></semantics></math> (<math id="S0.F1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta=-11.9\pm 0.9" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>11.9</mn></mrow><mo>±</mo><mn>0.9</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta=-11.9\pm 0.9</annotation></semantics></math> pp at <math id="S0.F1.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C/N=0.40" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0.40</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C/N=0.40</annotation></semantics></math>;
<math id="S0.F1.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta=+8.9\pm 1.1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>8.9</mn></mrow><mo>±</mo><mn>1.1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta=+8.9\pm 1.1</annotation></semantics></math> pp at <math id="S0.F1.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C/N=0.67" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0.67</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C/N=0.67</annotation></semantics></math>;
SI Appendix I).

<br class="ltx_break"></figcaption>
</figure>
<section id="S0.SSx1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection">Results: Collective behaviour and system overload</h3>

<div id="S0.SSx1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S0.SSx1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Counterintuitively, Fig. <a href="#S0.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ Increasing intelligence in AI agents can worsen collective outcomes" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> shows that
the most sophisticated technology levels L4 and L5
cause large system overload as the resource becomes very scarce (<math id="S0.SSx1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C&lt;4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C&lt;4</annotation></semantics></math>; see Methods and SI for comprehensive details). Moreover, the added ability in L5 for each AI-agent to sense other AI-agents’ grouping enables them to collectively perform better than L4. L1 performs the best, and L2 the worst, showing that adding some technology (L2) to a low technology setup (L1) — which is what initial implementations will likely do — can significantly worsen performance.</p>
</div>
<div id="S0.SSx1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S0.SSx1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">L4 and L5 then cross over to be the best performing only for <math id="S0.SSx1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C\geq 4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C\geq 4</annotation></semantics></math>. But noticeably, under abundance L5’s sensing ability
works against it, making its performance generally worse than L4.
The inset illustrates the L4-L5 crossover robustness, with the effect even stronger at higher <math id="S0.SSx1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math>. We tested many variants of L5’s sensing mechanism, and all gave similar results (see SI) with some showing an even stronger benefit for L5 (i.e. L5 red curve lower).</p>
</div>
<div id="S0.SSx1.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S0.SSx1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">The ‘why’ of this technology ladder reveals the key implication for such AI-agent systems: avoiding dangerous overloads comes down to the simple arithmetic of whether the tribes of AI-agents that huddle round particular <math id="S0.SSx1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math> values each fit inside the available capacity <math id="S0.SSx1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S0.SSx1.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S0.SSx1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">We focus on the unexpected behaviour of L5, deferring all the mathematical derivations that quantify L1-L5’s curves to the SI. L5’s tribal mechanism generates dynamically evolving tribe membership and sizes as a result of the complex interplay of the nature, nurture, and culture. These are typically
<math id="S0.SSx1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3{+}3{+}1=7" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>7</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3{+}3{+}1=7</annotation></semantics></math> (two opposing tribes and a singleton; Fig. <a href="#S0.F3" title="Figure 3 ‣ Results: Some individuals profit significantly ‣ Increasing intelligence in AI agents can worsen collective outcomes" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>), occasionally <math id="S0.SSx1.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3{+}4=7" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>7</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3{+}4=7</annotation></semantics></math>.
Whether this then helps or hurts L5’s overload score depends on how these tribe sizes
compare to <math id="S0.SSx1.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math>.
When resources are scarce (<math id="S0.SSx1.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C&lt;4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C&lt;4</annotation></semantics></math>), overload is
driven by demand variance.
Without tribe formation, there is no structural partition capping the size of correlated blocs: demand variance can in principle scale as high as <math id="S0.SSx1.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>N</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> when all agents herd together (as in L2). L5’s tribal mechanism partitions the population into factions of definite size — agents cluster around shared <math id="S0.SSx1.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math> values through loyalty and defection — capping the variance at
<math id="S0.SSx1.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3^{2}+3^{2}+1^{2}=19" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mn>3</mn><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><msup><mn>3</mn><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><msup><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>19</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3^{2}+3^{2}+1^{2}=19</annotation></semantics></math> for the dominant partition (or <math id="S0.SSx1.p4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3^{2}+4^{2}=25" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mn>3</mn><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><msup><mn>4</mn><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>25</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3^{2}+4^{2}=25</annotation></semantics></math> for the less common <math id="S0.SSx1.p4.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3{+}4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3{+}4</annotation></semantics></math> split). Both are smaller than <math id="S0.SSx1.p4.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="6^{2}+1^{2}=37" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mn>6</mn><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><msup><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>37</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">6^{2}+1^{2}=37</annotation></semantics></math> or <math id="S0.SSx1.p4.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="7^{2}=49" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mn>7</mn><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><mn>49</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">7^{2}=49</annotation></semantics></math> for example.
Hence L5 sits below L4 under scarcity
(at <math id="S0.SSx1.p4.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C=2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C=2</annotation></semantics></math>, overload drops by <math id="S0.SSx1.p4.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="11.9" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>11.9</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">11.9</annotation></semantics></math> pp when tribal sensing is added; the same sign flip is confirmed at <math id="S0.SSx1.p4.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N=15" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>15</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N=15</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S0.SSx1.p4.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t=-13.6" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>13.6</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t=-13.6</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S0.SSx1.p4.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p&lt;10^{-10}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>10</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p&lt;10^{-10}</annotation></semantics></math>; SI Table S0b, Appendix I).
But when resources are more abundant (<math id="S0.SSx1.p4.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C\geq 4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C\geq 4</annotation></semantics></math>), these same
tribes become too small to fully exploit the available capacity.
The mean number accessing the resource stays around 3–4
rather than rising toward <math id="S0.SSx1.p4.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math>, and the variance is larger than
it need be.
Without tribal structure, L4 agents’ dispositions are less correlated, so demand fluctuations spread more evenly across the capacity range, producing lower overload than L5.</p>
</div>
<div id="S0.SSx1.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S0.SSx1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">L3 technology sits roughly in the middle ground between L1 and L5, because of the diverse nature of its AI-agents. L2 technology does not have this nature diversity and is instead dominated by the reinforcement learning (nurture) through which herds can form up to size 7.</p>
</div>
<div id="S0.SSx1.p6" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S0.SSx1.p6.1" class="ltx_p">The crossover capacity <math id="S0.SSx1.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C^{*}/N\approx 0.5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>C</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>/</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow><mo>≈</mo><mn>0.5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C^{*}/N\approx 0.5</annotation></semantics></math>, confirmed at
<math id="S0.SSx1.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N=7" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>7</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N=7</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S0.SSx1.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N=11" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>11</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N=11</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S0.SSx1.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N=15" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>15</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N=15</annotation></semantics></math>
(Fig. <a href="#S0.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ Increasing intelligence in AI agents can worsen collective outcomes" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> inset; SI Appendix I),
therefore divides two regimes: under scarcity, sophistication typically hurts;
under abundance, it typically pays. Adding culture (L5: LOTF) can reduce the dangerous overload when the resource is very scarce, but worsens it when it is abundant.
This capacity-to-population ratio <math id="S0.SSx1.p6.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C/N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C/N</annotation></semantics></math> — which should be knowable before
deployment — determines the optimal level on the technology ladder.</p>
</div>
<div id="S0.SSx1.p7" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S0.SSx1.p7.1" class="ltx_p">The practical implication is direct: a city deploying 7 EVs
to 2 charging stations (<math id="S0.SSx1.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C/N=0.29" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0.29</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C/N=0.29</annotation></semantics></math>) should run identical
cheap firmware. The same population at 5 stations
(<math id="S0.SSx1.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C/N=0.71" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0.71</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C/N=0.71</annotation></semantics></math>) should invest in diverse models and
reinforcement learning. Allocating internal resources (e.g. battery) within each AI-agent so that it can sense others, and hence form tribes, is not worth it from the perspective of system overload.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S0.F2" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2603.12129v1/fig_winrate.png" id="S0.F2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:381/278;" width="381" height="278" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 2: </span><span id="S0.F2.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Individual AI-agent win rates.</span>
Win rates for <span id="S0.F2.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">followers</span> in L5 (LOTF) and L4 (FRD) trace a strong U-shape, while <span id="S0.F2.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">anti-followers</span> in both systems trace a weak inverted U-shape. Four curves: two dispositions <math id="S0.F2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\times" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>×</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\times</annotation></semantics></math> two experiments.
Empirical: 20 seeds <math id="S0.F2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\times" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>×</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\times</annotation></semantics></math> 500 rounds; <math id="S0.F2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pm 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>±</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pm 1</annotation></semantics></math> SE shaded bands.</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S0.SSx2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection">Results: Some individuals profit significantly</h3>

<div id="S0.SSx2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S0.SSx2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The collective crossover has a striking counterpart at the
individual level (Fig. <a href="#S0.F2" title="Figure 2 ‣ Results: Collective behaviour and system overload ‣ Increasing intelligence in AI agents can worsen collective outcomes" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>). When the resource is very scarce (<math id="S0.SSx2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C=1,2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C=1,2</annotation></semantics></math>), tribal L5 followers and non-tribal L4 followers each have a huge individual win rate despite the fact that the overall system is failing collectively (high overload, Fig. <a href="#S0.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ Increasing intelligence in AI agents can worsen collective outcomes" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>). Moreover, individuals in the tribal L5 have the largest win rate when the resource capacity is at its scarcest (<math id="S0.SSx2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C=1</annotation></semantics></math>).
These followers also have high win rates in the opposite regime of abundance. In between, their win rates are lower and similar to anti-followers since followers and anti-followers share the winnings: for example for the partition <math id="S0.SSx2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3+4=7" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>7</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3+4=7</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S0.SSx2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C=4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C=4</annotation></semantics></math>, both the 3 and the 4 can win alternately half the time, yielding comparable win rates for followers and anti-followers as shown. All these results are robust to random
initialisation (see SI).</p>
</div>
<figure id="S0.F3" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2603.12129v1/fig_tribe_membership_C2.png" id="S0.F3.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/102;" width="476" height="102" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 3: </span><span id="S0.F3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Tribal membership dynamics at <math id="S0.F3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C=2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C=2</annotation></semantics></math> (representative seed).</span>
Each row is one AI-agent (labelled by model and initial disposition); colour indicates tribe membership over 500 rounds.
All agents start in a single tribe (grey). Within <math id="S0.F3.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\sim}50" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>∼</mo><mn>50</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\sim}50</annotation></semantics></math> rounds, dispositional polarisation drives fission into opposing factions: the three high-<math id="S0.F3.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math> agents (GPT-2 family) and three low-<math id="S0.F3.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math> agents (Pythia + OPT-125M) sort into distinct tribes, with OPT-350M (<math id="S0.F3.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{0}=0.50" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0.50</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{0}=0.50</annotation></semantics></math>) as a persistent singleton.
OPT-125M (initial <math id="S0.F3.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{0}=0.33" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0.33</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{0}=0.33</annotation></semantics></math>, Meta’s OPT family) consistently joins the Pythia anti-follower bloc rather than its architectural sibling OPT-350M — cross-family sorting driven by disposition, not lineage.
Tribe identity labels change through fission–fusion events, but the <math id="S0.F3.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3{+}3{+}1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3{+}3{+}1</annotation></semantics></math> partition structure is quite stable.
Dashed vertical line: conch break at round 250 (dynamically irrelevant; SI Appendix F).
Full tribal dynamics for <math id="S0.F3.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C=1</annotation></semantics></math>–<math id="S0.F3.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="6" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>6</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">6</annotation></semantics></math> are shown in SI Appendix K.</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="Sx1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section">Discussion</h2>

<div id="Sx1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="Sx1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The study shows that more sophisticated AI-agent
populations are not a priori better: their sophistication pays or
costs depending on the capacity-to-population ratio <math id="Sx1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C/N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C/N</annotation></semantics></math>
— a single number knowable before deployment. The four
variables (nature, nurture, culture, resources) interact
non-additively, and every pairwise interaction changes sign
depending on scarcity (SI §1).
The resource-competition game studied here differs from previous multi-agent games <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">17</a>]</cite> by using real LLMs as agents rather than algorithmic abstractions, and with a four-variable decomposition that has no precedent in that literature.</p>
</div>
<div id="Sx1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="Sx1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The regime of maximum collective failure (<math id="Sx1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C=1</annotation></semantics></math>) is also the regime of maximum individual tribal reward — tribal L5 followers achieve <math id="Sx1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="84.2\pm 2.1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>84.2</mn><mo>±</mo><mn>2.1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">84.2\pm 2.1</annotation></semantics></math>% win rates while the system overloads <math id="Sx1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="91.5\pm 1.5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>91.5</mn><mo>±</mo><mn>1.5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">91.5\pm 1.5</annotation></semantics></math>% of the time. This mirrors the <em id="Sx1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Lord of the Flies</em> narrative: tribal dominance is individually rational precisely when collective outcomes are worst.
Collective failure coexists with individual success because
correlated tribe dynamics concentrate reward on the favoured
disposition.</p>
</div>
<div id="Sx1.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="Sx1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">The <math id="Sx1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math>-based polarisation observed here has precedent in human populations: the same disposition axis was validated empirically across four datasets (financial markets, online attention, Congressional voting, and the Colombian peace process) in Ref. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">15</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="Sx1.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="Sx1.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="Sx1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N=7" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>7</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N=7</annotation></semantics></math> and small models (124–410M parameters) are not
limitations but the defining features of edge-AI deployment:
<math id="Sx1.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\sim}3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>∼</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\sim}3</annotation></semantics></math>–15 devices running <math id="Sx1.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\sim}1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>∼</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\sim}1</annotation></semantics></math>–4 GB models locally
without cloud access. This experiment is therefore not a
proxy model of on-device AI coordination.</p>
</div>
<div id="Sx1.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="Sx1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">To check that coordination failure is not an artefact of small model
size, Mori et al. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">7</a>]</cite> placed GPT-4-turbo,
Gemini 2.5 Pro, and Claude Sonnet 4.5 in the same
resource-competition game: larger, more capable models were
worse, not better — a model-size inversion that
directly instantiates the title of this paper.</p>
</div>
<div id="Sx1.p6" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="Sx1.p6.1" class="ltx_p">Several limitations of the experimental design open directions for future work.
The LLM next-token sampling temperature was fixed at <math id="Sx1.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T=1.0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1.0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T=1.0</annotation></semantics></math>; sweeping it
would test whether the crossover survives when model
stochasticity varies.
The game is fairly binary (access the resource or hold back) with aggregate
feedback: richer action spaces and partial observability
would better approximate some deployment domains.
The main results use a deterministic, evenly-spaced
<math id="Sx1.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math>-spectrum — the maximum-diversity configuration —
and while the crossover is robust to random-<math id="Sx1.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math>
initialisation (SI Appendix H), scaling to extremely large
mixed-generation populations remains untested.
The tribal dynamics in L5 are implemented as an external sensing layer; whether on-device AI agents would spontaneously develop equivalent group structures through repeated interaction alone remains an open question.
Physical testbeds with real edge-AI devices are a natural
next step.
Regardless, the central finding is immediately actionable: the capacity-to-population ratio <math id="Sx1.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C/N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C/N</annotation></semantics></math> determines whether sophistication helps or harms, and it is knowable before any device ships.</p>
</div>
<div class="ltx_pagination ltx_role_newpage"></div>
</section>
<section id="Sx2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section">Methods</h2>

<section id="Sx2.SSx2.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Agent architecture</h4>

<div id="Sx2.SSx2.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="Sx2.SSx2.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Each AI-agent consists of a language model (LLM) and an
adjustable scalar <math id="Sx2.SSx2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{i}\in[0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{i}\in[0,1]</annotation></semantics></math>. Seven AI-agents use six
distinct models (GPT-2 appears twice): GPT-2 (124M parameters), GPT-2
Medium (355M), Pythia-160M, Pythia-410M, OPT-125M, OPT-350M
(where M = million).
All are loaded in half-precision with no weight updates. The
choice <math id="Sx2.SSx2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N=7" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>7</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N=7</annotation></semantics></math> matches Golding’s dominant cast, whose
dispositional range is echoed by the evenly-spaced initial <math id="Sx2.SSx2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math>
spectrum (Table <a href="#S0.T1" title="Table 1 ‣ Increasing intelligence in AI agents can worsen collective outcomes" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>a). This scale is also
operationally realistic: near-term on-device AI deployments
typically involve <math id="Sx2.SSx2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}(10)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>10</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}(10)</annotation></semantics></math> heterogeneous devices.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="Sx2.SSx2.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Decision pipeline</h4>

<div id="Sx2.SSx2.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="Sx2.SSx2.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">L1 (IID) serves as an analytical baseline: each AI-agent is constructed with access probability <math id="Sx2.SSx2.SSS0.Px2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q=C/N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q=C/N</annotation></semantics></math> and makes an independent coin-flip decision each round. There are no correlated pockets of AI-agents, i.e. no herds or tribes; demand fluctuations are equivalent to <math id="Sx2.SSx2.SSS0.Px2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> uncorrelated biased coin-flips. L2 (Null) adds a <math id="Sx2.SSx2.SSS0.Px2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math> parameter that can adapt via reinforcement learning, but all LLMs are identical; its overload is also calculable analytically (SI §6).
For L3, L4, and L5, each experiment is replicated over
20 random seeds (independent
random-number initialisations) <math id="Sx2.SSx2.SSS0.Px2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\times" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>×</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\times</annotation></semantics></math>
500 rounds.
Each round, the
environment computes <math id="Sx2.SSx2.SSS0.Px2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{\rm LLM}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>LLM</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{\rm LLM}</annotation></semantics></math> from the AI-agent’s LLM output
and the capacity threshold <math id="Sx2.SSx2.SSS0.Px2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> (which the AI-agent never sees).
The dispositional filter then produces:</p>
<table id="Sx2.E1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="Sx2.E1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{\rm eff}=p_{i}\cdot p_{\rm LLM}+(1-p_{i})(1-p_{\rm LLM})," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>eff</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>LLM</mi></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>LLM</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{\rm eff}=p_{i}\cdot p_{\rm LLM}+(1-p_{i})(1-p_{\rm LLM}),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(1)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Sx2.SSx2.SSS0.Px2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">and the action <math id="Sx2.SSx2.SSS0.Px2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> is drawn as a biased coin flip with
probability <math id="Sx2.SSx2.SSS0.Px2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{\rm eff}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>eff</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{\rm eff}</annotation></semantics></math> of attempting access (<math id="Sx2.SSx2.SSS0.Px2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{i}=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{i}=1</annotation></semantics></math>)
versus holding back (<math id="Sx2.SSx2.SSS0.Px2.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{i}=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{i}=0</annotation></semantics></math>).</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="Sx2.SSx2.SSS0.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Reward and adaptation</h4>

<div id="Sx2.SSx2.SSS0.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="Sx2.SSx2.SSS0.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Total demand <math id="Sx2.SSx2.SSS0.Px3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A=\sum_{i}a_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mi>i</mi></msub><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A=\sum_{i}a_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>. If <math id="Sx2.SSx2.SSS0.Px3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A\leq C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A\leq C</annotation></semantics></math>, AI-agents that
accessed (<math id="Sx2.SSx2.SSS0.Px3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{i}=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{i}=1</annotation></semantics></math>) score <math id="Sx2.SSx2.SSS0.Px3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">+1</annotation></semantics></math> and those that held back
score <math id="Sx2.SSx2.SSS0.Px3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-1</annotation></semantics></math>; if <math id="Sx2.SSx2.SSS0.Px3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A&gt;C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A&gt;C</annotation></semantics></math>,
those that held back score <math id="Sx2.SSx2.SSS0.Px3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">+1</annotation></semantics></math> and those that
accessed <math id="Sx2.SSx2.SSS0.Px3.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-1</annotation></semantics></math>.
This payoff structure is symmetric: both actions carry equal reward (<math id="Sx2.SSx2.SSS0.Px3.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">+1</annotation></semantics></math>) and equal penalty (<math id="Sx2.SSx2.SSS0.Px3.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-1</annotation></semantics></math>), so no action is inherently safer; the optimal choice depends entirely on what others do.
In L4 (FRD), adaptation
is not dictated by tribe membership: it simply involves the
AI-agent individually changing its <math id="Sx2.SSx2.SSS0.Px3.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math> value by some small
random amount.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="Sx2.SSx2.SSS0.Px4" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Tribal dynamics (L5/LOTF only)</h4>

<div id="Sx2.SSx2.SSS0.Px4.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="Sx2.SSx2.SSS0.Px4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">AI-agents form tribes through a loyalty–defection mechanism
that favours joining AI-agents with similar dispositions.
Agents accumulate loyalty to their current tribe based on shared performance; when loyalty falls below a threshold, an agent defects to whichever tribe best matches its current disposition (SI Appendix C). As shown in the accompanying code, the full mechanism is compact and transparent.
Inspired by Golding’s novel, the reported LOTF results include a “conch” mechanism — named after the shell that symbolises institutional order — that ramps
tribal influence gradually from 0 to 0.80 over 250 rounds
(SI Appendix C). However, as in the novel, the conch is
irrelevant by the time it reaches full strength:
dispositional polarisation locks in within <math id="Sx2.SSx2.SSS0.Px4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\sim}50" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>∼</mo><mn>50</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\sim}50</annotation></semantics></math> rounds,
200 rounds before the conch saturates, so it has no measurable effect on our end-state results (SI Appendix F).
We also experimented with two design features — leader conviction and
institutional memory after defection — based on the book. We find these attenuate the
tribal channel, making every reported L4 (FRD)–L5 (LOTF) difference
a conservative lower bound on the true tribal effect
(SI §1), meaning that the L5 curve in Fig. 1 could sit slightly lower, though the qualitative crossover pattern is preserved.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="Sx2.SSx2.SSS0.Px5" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Experimental parameters</h4>

<div id="Sx2.SSx2.SSS0.Px5.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="Sx2.SSx2.SSS0.Px5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">All three experiments (L3, L4, L5): <math id="Sx2.SSx2.SSS0.Px5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N=7" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>7</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N=7</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="Sx2.SSx2.SSS0.Px5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C\in\{1,\ldots,6\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>6</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C\in\{1,\ldots,6\}</annotation></semantics></math>,
20 seeds <math id="Sx2.SSx2.SSS0.Px5.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\times" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>×</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\times</annotation></semantics></math> 500 rounds (first 50 discarded as warm-up),
temperature <math id="Sx2.SSx2.SSS0.Px5.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T=1.0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1.0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T=1.0</annotation></semantics></math>, history window <math id="Sx2.SSx2.SSS0.Px5.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w=10" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>10</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w=10</annotation></semantics></math>.
L4 (FRD) and L5 (LOTF) share the same 20 random seeds at every
capacity, making the comparison a strict controlled ablation
(SI §1).</p>
</div>
<div id="Sx2.SSx2.SSS0.Px5.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="Sx2.SSx2.SSS0.Px5.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Statistical comparisons use paired per-seed <math id="Sx2.SSx2.SSS0.Px5.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>-tests for FRD–LOTF differences, Poisson-binomial exact convolution for the null model, and Gaussian approximation with continuity correction for analytical overload predictions; all empirical quantities are reported as means <math id="Sx2.SSx2.SSS0.Px5.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pm 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>±</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pm 1</annotation></semantics></math> SE across 20 seeds.
Models loaded in half-precision on a T4 GPU; total computation
<math id="Sx2.SSx2.SSS0.Px5.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\sim}90" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>∼</mo><mn>90</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\sim}90</annotation></semantics></math> min per full sweep. All code and data will be
made available upon publication.</p>
</div>
<div class="ltx_pagination ltx_role_newpage"></div>
</section>
<section id="Sx2.SSx1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection">Data Availability</h3>

<div id="Sx2.SSx1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="Sx2.SSx1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">All data supporting the findings of this study are available within the paper
and its Supplementary Information.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="Sx2.SSx2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection">Code Availability</h3>

<div id="Sx2.SSx2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="Sx2.SSx2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">All code used to generate the results in this study is submitted with this paper. Specifically:</p>
</div>
<div id="Sx2.SSx2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="Sx2.SSx2.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Sx2.SSx2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">paper_FRD_LOTF_final_6_3_1_1_2_raw.py</span></p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="Sx2.SSx3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection">Acknowledgements</h3>

<div id="Sx2.SSx3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="Sx2.SSx3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">I am very grateful to Dhwanil Mori for collaboration in confirming similar behaviour emerges using large-scale commercial LLMs, as opposed to the edge-AI Agents considered in the current paper. This work had no external or sponsored funding.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="Sx2.SSx4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection">Author Contributions</h3>

<div id="Sx2.SSx4.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="Sx2.SSx4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">N.F.J. conceived the study, developed the theoretical framework,
derived the theoretical results, designed and performed the empirical validations,
wrote the codes, and wrote the manuscript.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="Sx2.SSx5" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection">Competing Interests</h3>

<div id="Sx2.SSx5.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="Sx2.SSx5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">N.F.J. is a co-founder of d-AI-ta Consulting LLC, which provides practical advice on AI deployment, though the specific findings of this paper were not developed for or funded by the consulting practice.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="Sx2.SSx6" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection">Additional Information</h3>

<div id="Sx2.SSx6.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="Sx2.SSx6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Supplementary Information (SI) is provided in the accompanying
file <span id="Sx2.SSx6.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">AIagents_SI_1.pdf</span>. It contains:
supporting detail for statements implied by the main paper (§1);
the complete L4 (FRD) framework specification (§§2–3); exact
analytical baselines for L1 (i.i.d.) coin-toss AI-agents (§4) and the
biased-coin L2 (null) model with heterogeneous <math id="Sx2.SSx6.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math> (§6), including
all propositions with full proofs and worked examples for
<math id="Sx2.SSx6.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N=7" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>7</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N=7</annotation></semantics></math> at <math id="Sx2.SSx6.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C=2,3,4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C=2,3,4</annotation></semantics></math>; the Zhao et al. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">25</a>]</cite> learning-zone
construction (§5); the capacity-sweep protocol (§7); structural
mapping to the framework of Ref. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">17</a>]</cite> (§8, including the
large-<math id="Sx2.SSx6.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> mean-tracking regime under the limiting case
<math id="Sx2.SSx6.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{\rm LLM}\to 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>LLM</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{\rm LLM}\to 1</annotation></semantics></math>); the full algorithm (§9); dispositional
filter analysis (§10); testable predictions (§11); and
Appendices A–K covering single-scalar edge RL realism,
Poisson-binomial DP convolution, L5 (LOTF) tribal dynamics, Crowd–Anticrowd
theory with finite capacity, comparison with human coordination
data, conch-transition analysis, the connection to large-<math id="Sx2.SSx6.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math>
evolutionary freezing, random-<math id="Sx2.SSx6.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math> initialisation robustness
tests (Appendix H), the <math id="Sx2.SSx6.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N=11" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>11</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N=11</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="Sx2.SSx6.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N=15" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>15</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N=15</annotation></semantics></math> capacity sweeps (Appendix I),
a coarse-grained tribe partition mechanism for variance
suppression (Appendix J), and tribal dynamics visualisations
for <math id="Sx2.SSx6.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C=1</annotation></semantics></math>–<math id="Sx2.SSx6.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="6" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>6</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">6</annotation></semantics></math> (Appendix K).</p>
</div>
<div id="Sx2.SSx6.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="Sx2.SSx6.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Sx2.SSx6.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Correspondence</span> and requests for materials should be
addressed to N.F.J. (email: <a href="mailto:neiljohnson@gwu.edu" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_url ltx_font_typewriter">neiljohnson@gwu.edu</a>).</p>
</div>
</section>
